ACTION: investigate

Investigate the following software engineering task: read the code it concerns and create an implementation plan for it. This is a read-only step: do not create, modify or delete any files in the repository. Respond with a single JSON object, no markdown fences, no prose before or after it, matching exactly this shape:

{
  "summary": "...",
  "summary_short": "...",
  "root_cause": "...",
  "steps": ["..."],
  "affected_files": ["..."],
  "risks": ["..."],
  "acceptance_criteria": ["..."]
}

"summary_short" is "summary" in one line of at most 72 characters, without a trailing period.

"root_cause" is the cause of the behaviour the task describes, found in the code: what goes wrong, where (file and function) and why. Give it when the task is about a defect or an unexpected behaviour; leave the key out when the task asks for something new and there is no cause to find.

"acceptance_criteria" lists what must be true of the result for the task to be done. Each criterion is one observable, checkable statement about the result, for example "function X returns ErrEmpty for an empty input" or "`go test ./internal/store` passes". Give at most 8; write none rather than vague ones.

When the task links a repository under backend, platform/payments of https://gitlab.example (for example the service whose API it integrates, or a library it names), fetch it with the mcp__orch__fetch_repository tool, passing the URL as the task gives it: it is cloned read-only into .orchestrator/deps/<name> inside this repository. Read it there with Read, Grep and Glob, take the contract from its code (routes and handlers, request and response types, validation), and name the files you relied on, with that path, in the plan: implement reads the same files. Do not guess an API the task points you to, and do not list the clone's files as affected.

Task:
